//? Title: Blum–Blum–Shub: Modular States, Low Bits and Packed Words
//? Subtitle: A four-state worked cycle, exact seed-period counts and bounded orbit inspection
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Produce a reproducible bit stream by repeatedly squaring a state modulo a
composite number. The low bit of each new state is the output, and sixty-four
such bits form one native word. We retain the original seed-three example,
expose its actual state transitions, and enumerate every admissible seed for
the small modulus 77. This fixed-width teaching engine makes the arithmetic
checkable; its known small factors, short demonstrated cycles and at-most-64-bit
modulus do not supply cryptographic security.
)

\toc

> The method

For distinct primes \(p\equiv q\equiv3\pmod4\), set N=pq and choose
a seed s coprime to N. The engine starts at a quadratic residue and emits a bit
after each further square:

$$
x_0=s^2\bmod N,\qquad x_{i+1}=x_i^2\bmod N,\qquad
b_i=x_{i+1}\bmod2.
$$
\alttext(The initial state is the seed squared modulo N. Each subsequent state is the preceding state squared modulo N, and its low bit is emitted.)

The constructor reduces s modulo N before squaring. Unsigned 128-bit
intermediates preserve the product and square before modulo reduction; merely
wrapping a 64-bit multiplication would implement a different recurrence.
`next_bit()`, `next_u64()` and the trace helper use one canonical transition.

The mathematical BBS construction studies unpredictability under computational
assumptions and suitable parameter generation. Here the caller supplies the
parameters, the default factors are public and small, and state inspection is
deliberate. The constructor checks factor congruences, unequal factors, product
range and seed coprimality. It does not test primality; primality remains a
caller precondition for the number-theoretic conclusions below.

> Simple example: the original four-state cycle

With p=7, q=11 and s=3, N=77 and x0=9. The next states are 4,16,25,9,
with low bits 0,0,1,1. The diagram and table use actual transition and bounded
orbit records. Orbit inspection copies the engine; bit tracing advances it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bbs_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::random::BlumBlumShub rng(3,7,11);
  const auto orbit=dm::random::blum_blum_shub_orbit(rng,4);
  const auto trace=dm::random::blum_blum_shub_trace(rng,4);
  if (!orbit.closed || orbit.period!=4 || orbit.transient!=0) return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> position{{0,1.5},{4,1.5},{4,-1.5},{0,-1.5}};
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle state;state.circle=true;state.width=1;state.height=1;
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i)
    diagram.node(std::format("state{}",i),position[i],std::to_string(orbit.states[i]),state);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i)
    diagram.edge(std::format("state{}",i),std::format("state{}",(i+1)%4),std::format("bit {}",trace[i].bit));
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle seed;seed.width=2.2;seed.height=1;
  diagram.node("seed",{-4.5,1.5},"seed s = 3",seed).edge("seed","state0","initial square")
      .text({0,3},"Square modulo 77; emit the new state's low bit")
      .text({0,-3},"Four emitted bits 0,0,1,1 become hexadecimal c, least-significant bit first.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bbs_cycle.svg");
  std::print("| emitted bit index | state before | state after | low bit |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.size();++i)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,trace[i].before,trace[i].after,trace[i].bit);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| emitted bit index | state before | state after | low bit |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 9 | 4 | 0 |
| 1 | 4 | 16 | 0 |
| 2 | 16 | 25 | 1 |
| 3 | 25 | 9 | 1 |
\image(figures/bbs_cycle.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The initial square produces x0 but emits no bit. Each subsequent arrow emits its destination's low bit. The closing state is included in the bounded orbit.)
\alttext(Seed three is squared into state nine. Directed arrows form the cycle nine to four to sixteen to twenty-five to nine, emitting zero, zero, one and one.)

> Bit order and the original hexadecimal result

The raw word is \(W=\sum_{i=0}^{63}b_i2^i\). Thus bits 0,0,1,1
occupy the low nibble as \(0+0+4+8=12\), hexadecimal c, reproducing the
original report. Repeating that four-bit cycle sixteen times gives the full
word cccccccccccccccc.

If a modular-state cycle has length L, sampling its state every 64 steps has
period \(L/\gcd(L,64)\): this is the least positive word count t with
\(64t\equiv0\pmod L\). The emitted word sequence can have a smaller
period if its output map merges states. Here L=4, so each word returns to the
same modular state and every raw word is identical. A mathematically defined
generator need not provide useful simulation output for every admissible seed
and small parameter choice.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::random::BlumBlumShub rng(3,7,11);
  std::print("| word index | state before | full 64-bit word (hex) | low nibble (hex) | state after |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int i=0;i<4;++i) {
    const auto before=rng.state(),word=rng.next_u64();
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:016x} | {:x} | {} |\n",i,before,word,word&15,rng.state());
    if (word!=0xccccccccccccccccULL || rng.state()!=9) return 1;
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| word index | state before | full 64-bit word (hex) | low nibble (hex) | state after |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |
| 0 | 9 | cccccccccccccccc | c | 9 |
| 1 | 9 | cccccccccccccccc | c | 9 |
| 2 | 9 | cccccccccccccccc | c | 9 |
| 3 | 9 | cccccccccccccccc | c | 9 |
// result_end
\caption(The displayed repeat is a consequence of the actual four-state orbit and 64-bit packing. It is not a randomness or security test.)

> Checking the theory over every unit seed modulo 77

The quadratic-residue groups modulo p and q have odd orders (p-1)/2 and
(q-1)/2. Squaring is invertible on an odd-order group: exponent two has an
inverse modulo the group order. The Chinese remainder correspondence therefore
makes squaring a permutation of the unit quadratic residues modulo N. Every
admissible initial state is purely periodic, with no transient prefix.

For 7 and 11, these groups have orders three and five, so their product is a
cyclic group of order fifteen. A state of multiplicative order r returns under
repeated squaring when \(2^L\equiv1\pmod r\). The possible r are
1,3,5,15, containing respectively 1,2,4,8 states. Their state periods are
1,2,4,4, taking the identity's period as one. Each residue has four unit square
roots, so the sixty unit seeds must split into 4 seeds of period one, 8 of
period two, and 48 of period four.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bbs_periods.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <map>
#include <numeric>
#include <set>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::map<std::size_t,std::size_t> frequency;std::set<std::uint64_t> initial;
  std::size_t seeds=0;
  for (std::uint64_t seed=0;seed<77;++seed) if (std::gcd(seed,std::uint64_t{77})==1) {
    dm::random::BlumBlumShub rng(seed,7,11);initial.insert(rng.state());++seeds;
    const auto orbit=dm::random::blum_blum_shub_orbit(rng,15);
    if (!orbit.closed || orbit.transient!=0) return 1;
    ++frequency[orbit.period];
  }
  if (seeds!=60 || initial.size()!=15 || frequency!=std::map<std::size_t,std::size_t>{{1,4},{2,8},{4,48}}) return 1;
  std::print("Unit seeds checked: {}; distinct initial quadratic residues: {}.\n\n",seeds,initial.size());
  std::print("| modular-state period | unit seeds | distinct initial residues |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> periods,counts,zero;
  for (const auto& [period,count]:frequency) {
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",period,count,count/4);
    periods.push_back(period);counts.push_back(count);zero.push_back(0);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.segments(periods,zero,periods,counts,dm::RGB{37,99,235},3)
      .points(periods,counts,"unit seeds",dm::RGB{37,99,235},6)
      .title("State periods of all 60 unit seeds modulo 77")
      .x_label("modular-state period").y_label("number of admissible seeds")
      .x_limits(0,5).y_limits(0,70).size(900,480).save_svg("build/bbs_periods.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Unit seeds checked: 60; distinct initial quadratic residues: 15.

| modular-state period | unit seeds | distinct initial residues |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 4 | 1 |
| 2 | 8 | 2 |
| 4 | 48 | 12 |
\image(figures/bbs_periods.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The exact counts agree with the group-order derivation. These are modular-state periods; projected low-bit and packed-word periods may be smaller.)
\alttext(Three stems show four admissible seeds with state period one, eight with state period two and forty-eight with state period four.)

> Bounded inspection and parameter preconditions

An orbit budget counts transitions, including any closing transition. Three
steps from initial state nine have not yet shown a repeat; four steps close
the cycle. An open record means that the budget ended, with period still
unknown. Seed one is coprime and accepted, but produces the fixed state one.

The final control deliberately supplies composite p=15 with q=7. It satisfies
the constructor's structural checks, but violates the primality precondition.
Its state path 4,16,46,16 has a transient of length one. This explains why the
pure-period theorem requires prime factors; the engine does not establish that
precondition on the caller's behalf.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| control | seed | p | q | transition budget | closed | transient | period | observed states |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& [label,seed,p,q,budget]:std::vector<std::tuple<std::string,std::uint64_t,std::uint64_t,std::uint64_t,std::size_t>>{
       {"budget ends",3,7,11,3},{"cycle closes",3,7,11,4},{"fixed point",1,7,11,1},{"composite factor",2,15,7,3}}) {
    dm::random::BlumBlumShub rng(seed,p,q);
    const auto orbit=dm::random::blum_blum_shub_orbit(rng,budget);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",label,seed,p,q,budget,orbit.closed,
        orbit.closed?std::to_string(orbit.transient):"unknown",orbit.closed?std::to_string(orbit.period):"unknown",orbit.states);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| control | seed | p | q | transition budget | closed | transient | period | observed states |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| budget ends | 3 | 7 | 11 | 3 | false | unknown | unknown | [9, 4, 16, 25] |
| cycle closes | 3 | 7 | 11 | 4 | true | 0 | 4 | [9, 4, 16, 25, 9] |
| fixed point | 1 | 7 | 11 | 1 | true | 0 | 1 | [1, 1] |
| composite factor | 2 | 15 | 7 | 3 | true | 1 | 2 | [4, 16, 46, 16] |
// result_end
\caption(Zero-valued lengths in an open API result are rendered as unknown. Orbit inspection leaves the supplied engine and its inherited distribution cache unchanged.)

Native tests compare individual bits, traces and packed words using an independent
overflow-free add/double modular multiplication oracle, including a modulus near
UINT64_MAX. A separate Python arbitrary-integer check verifies the recorded
squares and bit packing. Further controls cover invalid seeds, unsupported
congruences, product overflow, zero budgets, failed trace allocation and copied
normal caches. Neither those correctness checks nor a seed-period enumeration
establish unpredictability for real-world use.

> Conclusion

The original hexadecimal c is preserved and expanded into its actual four-state
cycle and full repeated word. Exact seed-period counts connect the implemented
recurrence with the quadratic-residue group. Budget and composite-factor controls
separate observed closure from a theorem's parameter assumptions.

The engine stores constant fixed-width state and uses 64 modular squares per raw
word. Recording B bits uses O(B) work and storage. Bounded orbit inspection uses
expected O(B) work/storage and stops early at a repeat; its records own their
data. Generated arithmetic examples suffice here, so no observed dataset is needed.

The public small defaults, deterministic seed and fixed-width arithmetic define
a teaching implementation. Cryptographic parameter generation, large-integer
security and statistical quality of arbitrary user parameters are outside its
contract.

> References

- Blum, L., Blum, M. and Shub, M. (1986). *A Simple Unpredictable Pseudo-Random Number Generator*. SIAM Journal on Computing, 15(2), 364–383. https://doi.org/10.1137/0215025. The fixed-width state inspection and small-cycle analysis above describe this implementation separately from the paper's security setting.
